# showcase.mml -- an MML feature showcase for EMU16's music engine.
#
# This isn't meant to be a great song -- it exists so every MML language
# feature shows up at least once, with a comment on nearly every line
# explaining exactly what that line does and why. Compile + listen with:
#     python tools/mml.py assets/music/showcase.mml --preview
# (needs `make song_render` once, first time -- see docs/tools.md#mmlpy).
#
# Two things you will NOT find here, because they don't exist at the MML
# level (only in the raw lib/apu.lib / .song format underneath it):
#   - portamento/slide (note_slide) -- no MML syntax calls this yet.
#   - multiple patterns or a custom order list -- v1 of the .song format is
#     always exactly one pattern that loops to itself; tools/mml.py always
#     writes order = [0, 255] for you. One long pattern IS the whole song.

# ---- TEMPO ------------------------------------------------------------
# `speed N` would be a one-line tempo: every row takes exactly N frame-ticks.
# `groove` is the richer form used here: a table of per-row frame counts
# that CYCLES, so rows don't all take equal time -- that's swing/shuffle
# instead of perfectly mechanical timing. This 2-entry table alternates a
# 7-frame row then a 5-frame row, forever (average tempo ~= speed 6).
groove 7 5

# ---- INSTRUMENTS --------------------------------------------------------
# An instrument is nothing but three optional per-frame-tick "macro" tables
# (vol/duty/arp) plus an optional vibrato and a noise mode. Every macro
# either STOPS (no `|` anywhere -> hold the last value forever) or LOOPS (a
# `|` marks the index playback jumps back to once it runs off the end).
# WHERE you put the `|` changes the shape completely -- "lead" vs "shimmer"
# and "stab" vs "shimmer" below are the two ends of that spectrum.

inst lead
    # Attack rises 8 -> 12 -> 15, then `|` marks index 3 (the "13" right
    # after it) as the loop point: the macro plays 8,12,15,13 once, then
    # jumps back to index 3 and just replays "13" forever. Net shape: a
    # quick attack settling into a sustained volume -- an "organ" envelope.
    vol 8 12 15 | 13
    # No `|` at all here -> duty sweeps 1 -> 2 -> 3 once, then HOLDS at 3
    # (the widest/brightest pulse) forever. A one-shot brightening attack.
    duty 1 2 3
    # vib DEPTH SPEED DELAY. depth is in the same 8.8 fixed-point units as
    # pitch (256 = one semitone), so 32 is a gentle ~1/8-semitone waver, not
    # a cartoon wobble. delay=12 means 12 frame-ticks (12/60s @ 60Hz) of
    # dead-straight pitch before vibrato kicks in -- real singers and
    # violinists do the same thing, they don't shake from the first instant.
    vib 32 8 12

inst pluck
    # No loop -> decays 15 -> 9 -> 4 -> 0 once and then STAYS at 0 (silent).
    # That silence-at-the-end is what makes this read as a short "pluck"
    # instead of a sustained tone, purely from the volume macro's shape.
    vol 15 9 4 0
    # A single value with no loop is the simplest possible macro: duty is
    # just fixed at 0 (the thinnest pulse) for the instrument's whole life.
    duty 0

inst bass
    # Same "decay to silence" idea as pluck, just a touch longer -- gives
    # the bass a plucked, percussive character instead of a held tone.
    vol 15 11 7 4 0
    # Putting `|` FIRST, before any value, sets the loop point to index 0 --
    # the loop covers the ENTIRE macro, so it just cycles 1,3,1,3,... from
    # the start, forever. That's the idiom for "this whole macro repeats
    # indefinitely", as opposed to "play once then hold the tail" above.
    # Alternating a narrow (1) and wide (3) duty each tick gives the bass a
    # gritty, buzzing PWM-like timbre instead of one static pulse shape.
    duty | 1 3

inst stab
    vol 15 12 10 | 10   # attack then sustain-hold at 10 -- same idiom as "lead"'s vol
    duty 0              # fixed thin pulse (arp voices care about pitch, not duty, but it's cheap to set)
    # A MAJOR triad written as semitone offsets: root, +4, +7. The `|` comes
    # AFTER all three offsets, with "7" repeated once more after it -- so
    # the loop point is the LAST value. Net effect: the chord rolls
    # root -> third -> fifth ONCE, then holds on the fifth. A quick "stab".
    arp 0 4 7 | 7

inst shimmer
    vol 8 10 12 | 12    # soft attack, sustains quietly -- meant to sit under the lead, not over it
    duty 0
    # A MINOR triad: root, +3, +7. This time `|` comes FIRST, before any
    # value -- the whole macro loops, so it plays root->third->fifth, then
    # root->third->fifth again, forever, for as long as the note holds.
    # This is the "sustained shimmering chord" trick: trigger ONE long note
    # and let this macro do all the movement underneath it by itself.
    arp | 0 3 7

inst kick
    noise 0            # mode 0 = the full 15-bit hiss tap -- lower and more "thump"-like
    vol 15 9 4 0        # sharp decay to silence, no loop -- one hit, not a drone

inst snare
    noise 1             # mode 1 = the short/periodic tap -- brighter, metallic "snap"
    vol 15 10 6 3 0      # a slightly longer decay than the kick -- gives the snare more "body"

inst hat
    noise 1   # hats are bright too, so this reuses mode 1 (the kick alone is the odd one out tonally,
              # which is exactly why it's the one instrument that gets mode 0 above)
    vol 9 4 0  # quiet and very short -- a closed hi-hat "tick", not a hiss

# ---- DRUM SHORTCUTS -------------------------------------------------------
# `drum LETTER INST NOTE` lets a track use a single letter (chosen outside
# a-g, so it can never be confused with a pitched note) to trigger a
# specific instrument at a specific MIDI note. Octave has NO effect on
# these -- a drum letter always plays at exactly the note number given
# here, so setting o4/o5/etc. on a drum channel is a harmless no-op.
drum k kick 30
drum s snare 50
drum h hat 70

# ---- CH0: LEAD MELODY ------------------------------------------------------
# The header line only names the channel's default instrument ("lead");
# every note lives on the continuation lines below so each line can carry
# its own comment. Blank lines and full-line comments never break a
# track's continuation -- only recognized directive keywords do.
ch0 lead

    o5 c4 d8 e8    # o5 = octave 5 (one above middle C). c4 = quarter C5; d8+e8 = two eighths D5,E5
    f4. g8         # f4. = DOTTED quarter (1.5x an ordinary quarter) F5, then eighth G5 -- 1 whole note so far

    c+4 a-8        # c+4 = quarter C#5 (sharp, `+`/`#` both work); a-8 = eighth Ab5 (flat, `-`)
    e12 f12 g12    # three EIGHTH-TRIPLETS (length 12) back to back -- fills exactly one quarter-note beat
    > c8 <         # `>` = octave up (now o6) for one eighth C6, then `<` brings it straight back to o5
    l8 a b         # `l8` sets the DEFAULT note length to an eighth; "a" and "b" then need no length digit

    @pluck         # mid-track instrument switch -- everything from here plays on "pluck", not "lead"
    [c8 r8]2       # a repeat bracket: "c8 r8" (a staccato note, then a rest) played twice back to back
    d4 r4          # a plain quarter note, then a quarter REST -- closes the phrase exactly on the beat

# ---- CH1: BASS --------------------------------------------------------------
ch1 bass

    o2 [c8 c8 g8 g8]2   # o2 = octave 2 (down in bass range). Same [ ]N syntax as ch0, now on a
                        # genuine "walking" rhythm -- proof it works for any note content, not just rests

    f6 g6 a6            # three QUARTER-TRIPLETS (length 6) -- three notes fill a half note, not one beat
    f8 f8 f4             # two eighths then a quarter -- fills out the rest of this whole-note measure

    d16 d16 d16 d16      # four SIXTEENTH notes (length 16) -- a quick flourish
    c16 c16 c16 c16      # four more sixteenths
    g4 r4                # a quarter note, then a quarter rest -- closes the bassline exactly on the beat

# ---- CH2: CHORDS / PAD -------------------------------------------------------
ch2 stab

    o3 c2 c2      # o3 = octave 3 (chord register, between bass and lead). Two HALF notes on the same
                  # pitch: each retriggers "stab"'s roll-once arpeggio from scratch, since a fresh
                  # NOTE_ON_INST always resets a macro back to its first value -- so you get two quick
                  # "chord stabs" a half-note apart, not one continuous roll.

    @shimmer a1   # switch instruments, then ONE whole note. Because "shimmer"'s arp macro loops from
                  # its very first value, this single held note is enough for the whole root-third-fifth
                  # cycle to repeat underneath it for the entire whole note -- the "sustained shimmering
                  # chord" effect.

    @stab f4 f4 g4 g4   # back to the roll-once-and-hold instrument, now as four quick quarter-note
                        # stabs instead of half notes -- same instrument, a completely different rhythm

# ---- CH3: DRUMS ---------------------------------------------------------------
ch3 unused   # every note on this channel is a drum LETTER (not an a-g pitch), and drum letters carry
             # their own instrument via the `drum` lines above -- so this default-instrument name is
             # never actually consulted. Any placeholder word here is harmless; "unused" says so plainly.

    [[k16 h16]2 s8]2 r4   # NESTED repeat brackets: the inner [k16 h16]2 doubles a fast kick-hat pair;
                          # the outer *2 doubles that whole snare-punctuated group again -- a small
                          # drum-fill shape from four characters of bracket syntax. Then a quarter rest.

    [k8 h8 s8 h8]2        # the familiar boom-chick-snap-chick pattern, written once and repeated
                          # instead of typed out twice by hand

    h12 h12 h12 r8 k8      # three EIGHTH-TRIPLET hats (a quick flutter), an eighth rest, then a kick
    h16 h16 h16 h16 k4     # four sixteenth hats, then a closing quarter kick -- lands back on beat
                          # one exactly, for a seamless loop back to the top of the pattern

# ---- FEATURE CHECKLIST (everything above, for quick reference) --------------
# groove (swing table)                     | ch2: whole/half notes (c2, a1)
# vol macro: attack-then-hold  (lead/stab) | ch2: @inst mid-track switch (again)
# vol macro: decay-to-silence  (pluck/bass)| ch3: drum letters (k/s/h), octave-is-a-no-op
# duty macro: one-shot-then-hold (lead)    | ch3: single repeat bracket  [ ]N
# duty macro: infinite loop      (bass)    | ch3: NESTED repeat brackets [[ ]N ]N
# arp macro: roll-once-then-hold (stab)    | note lengths: 4,8,16 (straight), 6,12 (triplets), dotted (.)
# arp macro: infinite cycle      (shimmer) | accidentals: c+ / c# (sharp), c- (flat)
# vib DEPTH SPEED DELAY          (lead)    | octave: o5 (absolute), > / < (relative)
# noise mode 0 vs 1              (kick vs snare/hat) | default length: l8, then bare notes
# ch0: @inst mid-track switch (lead->pluck)| rests: r4, r8
